module

public import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.ReductiveTorusCentralizerTerminology
public import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.GroupScheme.Semisimple
import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.GroupScheme.AffineNormalQuotientGeometry
import WeilConjectures.Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.Geometrically.Connected

/-! # A central maximal torus forces nilpotence

Source: Milne, Algebraic Groups (2017), Proposition 17.23, p.359, as used in
Proposition 17.28, p.360; Definition 6.34 and Remark 6.35, p.133
(blueprint: lem-sfb-central-maximal-torus-nilpotent).

The conclusion exhibits a finite descending central series of closed subgroup schemes,
with commutator containment on all algebra-valued points, and termination of the
scheme-theoretic derived series. No smoothness of the intermediate terms is required.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory
open scoped MonObj

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- A smooth connected affine algebraic group over an algebraically closed field with
central maximal torus is nilpotent, hence solvable in the scheme-theoretic sense.
Source: Milne, Proposition 17.23, p.359, Proposition 17.28, p.360, and Remark 6.35, p.133
(blueprint: lem-sfb-central-maximal-torus-nilpotent).

Proof:
1. The identity section and lem-sbc-connected-rational-point-geometrically-connected
   make H geometrically connected. Centrality makes S normal on all test algebras.
   By lem-sfb-affine-normal-quotient-geometry, the quotient q : H → Q = H/S is affine,
   finite type, smooth and connected, with kernel S, and q is faithfully flat and
   finitely presented.
2. By lem-sfb-quotient-borel-torus-images, q(S) is a maximal torus of Q. As S is the
   kernel, this image is trivial. Every torus contains the trivial subgroup, so Q has
   no nontrivial torus.
3. By lem-sfb-no-torus-unipotent, Q has a closed faithful matrix representation with
   unipotent k-points. Since k is algebraically closed these are the required geometric
   points in lem-sfb-geometric-unipotence-unitriangular-embedding, which gives a closed
   embedding Q → U_n for some n ≥ 1.
4. In strictly upper triangular matrices let J_r consist of entries of distance at
   least r above the diagonal, with J_r = 0 for r ≥ n. Then J_a J_b ⊆ J_(a+b).
   The closed subgroup schemes F_r = I + J_r satisfy F_1 = U_n and F_n = 1.
   For X ∈ J_1 and Y ∈ J_r, the commutator minus I equals
   (XY - YX)(I + X)⁻¹(I + Y)⁻¹ ∈ J_(r+1). Thus [U_n,F_r] ⊆ F_(r+1).
   The inverse is the finite geometric sum, so this works over every algebra,
   including nonreduced algebras (def-weil-closed-immersion,
   def-sc-group-scheme-subgroup).
5. Pull these closed subgroups back along H → Q → U_n. This gives a descending
   series from H to S with the same commutator containments. Append 1; centrality
   of S gives the last containment [H,S] = 1. Reindex to obtain H_0 through H_m.
   Since the containments hold on every algebra, they also hold after scalar
   extension and for the closed subgroup schemes generated by the commutators.
6. Inductively the j-th scheme-theoretic derived subgroup lies in H_j, since
   [D^j H,D^j H] ⊆ [H,H_j] ⊆ H_(j+1). Hence the derived series terminates.
   If n = 1 the series is H = S ⊇ 1. This covers the trivial group and rank-zero S. -/
theorem central_maximal_torus_nilpotent
    (k : Type u) [Field k] [IsAlgClosed k]
    (H S : Grp (Over (Spec (.of k)))) (iS : S ⟶ H)
    (haff : IsAffine H.X.left)
    (hft : LocallyOfFiniteType H.X.hom) (hqc : QuasiCompact H.X.hom)
    (hsm : Smooth H.X.hom) (hconn : ConnectedSpace H.X.left)
    (hmax : ReductiveTorusCentralizer.IsMaximalTorus iS)
    (hcentral : ∀ (A : Type u) [CommRing A] [Algebra k A]
      (s : StandardGroupSchemes.affine k A ⟶ S.X)
      (h : StandardGroupSchemes.affine k A ⟶ H.X), Commute (s ≫ iS.hom.hom) h) :
    (∃ (m : ℕ) (C : Fin (m + 1) → Grp (Over (Spec (.of k))))
      (i : ∀ j : Fin (m + 1), C j ⟶ H),
      (∀ j : Fin (m + 1), IsClosedSubgroupScheme (i j).hom.hom) ∧
      IsIso (i 0) ∧ Nonempty (C (Fin.last m) ≅ Grp.trivial _) ∧
      (∀ j : Fin m, ∃ l : C j.succ ⟶ C j.castSucc, l ≫ i j.castSucc = i j.succ) ∧
      ∀ (j : Fin m) (A : Type u) [CommRing A] [Algebra k A]
        (h : StandardGroupSchemes.affine k A ⟶ H.X)
        (c : StandardGroupSchemes.affine k A ⟶ (C j.castSucc).X),
        ∃ z : StandardGroupSchemes.affine k A ⟶ (C j.succ).X,
          z ≫ (i j.succ).hom.hom =
            h * (c ≫ (i j.castSucc).hom.hom) * h⁻¹ *
              (c ≫ (i j.castSucc).hom.hom)⁻¹) ∧
    IsSolvableGroupScheme H.X := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
